      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_RHEL_10_V1R2_STIG.zip"
      cmd         : "/sbin/sysctl kernel.kexec_load_disabled"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*kernel.kexec_load_disabled[\\s]*=[\\s]*1[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "RHEL-10-701060 - RHEL 10 must restrict exposed kernel pointer address access."
      info        : "Exposing kernel pointers (through procfs or \"seq_printf()\") exposes kernel writable structures, which may contain functions pointers. If a write vulnerability occurs in the kernel, allowing write access to any of this structure, the kernel can be compromised. This option disallows any program without the CAP_SYSLOG capability to get the addresses of kernel pointers by replacing them with \"0\".

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000132-GPOS-00067, SRG-OS-000433-GPOS-00192"
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to restrict exposed kernel pointer address access.

Create a drop-in if it does not already exist:

$ sudo vi /etc/sysctl.d/99-kernel_kptr_restrict.conf

Add the following to the file:

kernel.kptr_restrict = 1

Reload settings from all system configuration files with the following command:

$ sudo sysctl --system"
